10725 0 obj
  << /ActualText (At a Palantir user conference in Denver in 2024\054 something unusual happened\056 When CEO Alex Karp took the stage\054 the room felt less like a technology seminar than a political rally\056 Rather than walking through product features\054 Karp spent most of his time framing Palantir as critical infrastructure defending the technological superiority of Western democracy\056\n\nAfterward\054 the dominant conversation in online investor communities was not about product specs\056 It was about what worldview the company represents\056\n\nThat scene reveals something important\072 technology stocks are no longer consumed purely as financial instruments\056 Investors do not just calculate revenue and profit\056 They ask\054 often unconsciously\054 what narrative they are joining when they allocate capital\056 At that point\054 a layer of value appears in the price that traditional valuation models cannot account for\056 This is what we might call an affective premium \024 the conversion of psychological satisfaction and a sense of communal belonging into real capital value\056\n\nApple and Alphabet are perhaps the two most financially formidable companies on earth \024 one generating over \044100 billion in free cash flow every year\054 the other effectively owning the infrastructure of global digital advertising\056 Yet many investors treat both as sophisticated utilities\072 admirable machines\054 but not stories worth betting on\056 Google\031s Gemini is a case in point\056 For all its technical capability\054 investors tend to read it less as a historic AI breakthrough and more as a defensive upgrade \024 a way to protect the search\055and\055advertising engine that already prints money\056 There is no sense of civilizational stakes\056 The result\072 despite their scale\054 both companies trade at relatively conservative multiples\056\n\nPalantir and Tesla are a different story\056 As of March 2026\054 Apple and Alphabet trade at price\055to\055earnings ratios of 20\02330\056 Palantir and Tesla trade at 200\023300\056 That is not a difference in growth rates or earnings forecasts\056 It is a difference in narrative\056\n\nPalantir has anchored itself to a political story about defending the West\056 Tesla has claimed the civilizational narrative \024 electrified energy\054 space\054 humanoid robotics\054 the shape of the next century\056 Inside their investor communities\054 holding shares in these companies is not purely a financial act\056 It is a statement of values\056 It is a sense of participating in a transformation that matters\056 In this sense\054 an asset has shifted from being a claim on cash flow to being a claim on membership in a worldview\056)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 10726 0 R
     /Pg 5299 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

