10802 0 obj
  << /ActualText (assume investor relations and product marketing can run independently\054 or believe their current communications work is adequate\056 In an environment of asset oversupply and information saturation\054 that half\055understanding is dangerous\056 Narrative is no longer a messaging wrapper\056 It is a matter of actively designing the cognitive structure through which markets perceive and value a company \024 and failure to organize that function enterprise\055wide means good products and healthy financials can still result in chronic undervaluation\056\n\nThe most common specific mistake is conflating corporate\055level narrative with product\055level narrative\054 or managing them without deliberate alignment\056\n\nCorporate narrative answers the big question\072 why does this company need to exist\077 What structural transition is it reading\054 and how does it position itself as both a beneficiary and a designer of the change coming\077 Product narrative answers a different question\072 why should this specific customer adopt this specific offering right now \024 what problem does it solve\054 how does it feel to use\054 what does choosing it say about you\077\n\nThe first frame attracts capital and institutional trust\056 The second generates demand and conversion\056 When they pull in different directions\054 the company\031s message fragments rather than compounds\056 In an oversupply environment\054 fragmentation is a direct discount on valuation\056\n\nIn markets where products are similar\054 imitation is fast\054 and investment opportunities multiply daily\054 competitive advantage goes to whoever defines the problem first\054 offers the most persuasive interpretive frame\054 and lowers the decision cost for customers and investors\056 Narrative is not a peripheral factor in that competition\056 It is the cognitive infrastructure that makes choice possible\056 Markets don\031t move on facts alone\056 They move on frameworks for reading facts\056\n\nDesigning that framework at the enterprise level is not about writing a better tagline or sharpening marketing copy\056 It is a strategic exercise that connects the company\031s reason for being to the macro environment it operates in\056 What scarcity does the company resolve\077 What institutional gap\054 behavioral shift\054 technological transition\054 or generational change does it ride\077 Does it respond to demand\054 or does it rewrite the grammar of demand itself\077 Companies that have clear answers to those questions can provide their own reference frame when performance is volatile or external conditions shift\056 Companies that don\031t will find that strong financials alone cannot sustain a consistent premium\056\n\nBut macro narrative is not sufficient on its own\056 Product narrative must capture attention and convert it into action \024 and most companies make a second\054 related mistake here\056 They build the product first and layer meaning on afterward\054 assuming the marketing team can retrofit a story at launch\056 That sequence no longer works\056 Markets identify the gap between function and narrative quickly\054 and retrospectively attached messaging reads as inauthentic\056 Product narrative needs to be a design variable from the beginning \024 embedded in how the product triggers sensory responses\054 in what contexts it invites sharing\054 in what language allows for spontaneous reinterpretation\054 in which communities it can acquire symbolic meaning\056\n\nThis is the domain of cognitive architecture design\056 Companies are not just selling products\056 They are shaping how customers read the market and position )
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 10803 0 R
     /Pg 6526 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

